## Three findings that shape the build

1. **The AEE is not acceptance.** Deposit returns HTTP 200 + an AEE even when the file is structurally wrong. Acceptance is the certificat de conformité (code 11). Closing a filing on the AEE is how a French payroll misses a deadline showing green.
2. **Only unitary deposit is supported** (one `S20` block) — and violating it fails *asynchronously*, days later, after the deadline. The pre-flight assertion in P6 §6.3 is the only real defence.
3. **The inbound leg is statutorily mandatory.** The DGFiP withholding rate reaches payroll *only* as CRM 94; there is no rate API. No return leg = tax failure at scale.

Also: there's no OAuth2 — the API reuses the portal credential triplet with the password in clear in the body. The concentrateur model avoids per-client credential custody, which is why I recommended it.

